//
// S7 — THE SCRIPT DRIVES THE GESTURES, WITH NO SERVER AT ALL.
//
// `SCRIPTED_EDITOR.md` §2's third line, and it is short because the first two
// happened: every gesture is a FUNCTION in `hex_editor`, so a scene is a loop over
// a text file rather than a socket, a tick and a browser.
//
//     lib/hex_editor/      gesture(world, author, args) -> Ack
//     src/editor_server    socket  → gesture(...)
//     src/editor_run       script  → gesture(...)          ← this file
//
// ⚠ IT IS THE SAME SCRIPT THE SERVER TAKES. `tools/scripts/*.keys` was written for
// `tools/script.mjs`, which drives the editor over a socket; the same file drives
// this. That is the claim worth having — one format for authoring, replay and bug
// reports — and it is testable: build a scene both ways and the worlds must match.
//
// ⛔ AND IT WALKS NOW — plan 22 `T3`, [WALK_TICK.md](../doc/claude/WALK_TICK.md).
// The paragraph here used to say *"what it deliberately does NOT do is walk … a
// script that wanted one would be asking for a game rather than a scene"*, and the
// argument under it was sound and about the wrong thing: what a walking script wants
// is not a game, it is the **pad the footfalls lay**. There is no *flatten this area*
// message anywhere in the protocol, so `tools/scripts/deck.keys` cannot be built by
// any spelling that does not walk.
//
// ⚠ **A TICK, AND STILL NO CLOCK — THAT IS THE WHOLE LINE.** `step <n>` is exactly n
// `hex_editor::walk_tick` calls, and nothing else in this program can produce one.
// The server paces itself off the wall at `sim_rate` and the page off its own
// accumulator; this runner's answer stays a function of the script and of nothing
// else, which is its entire value. `rate` is still skipped, and there is nothing for
// it to set.
//
//   SCRIPT=tools/scripts/house.keys loft --lib lib/ src/editor_run.loft
//
// ⚠ THE SCRIPT COMES FROM THE ENVIRONMENT, as the server's port does. loft has no
// `fn main(argv)` today and `arguments` parses a vector somebody has to supply, so
// an env var is the honest way in rather than a wrapper pretending to be a CLI.
use hex_editor;
use hex_voxel;
use hex_grid;
// ⚠ FOR THE FALL, AND FOR NOTHING ELSE — plan 22 `T3`. `walk_tick` takes the ground
// as a FUNCTION because `hex_mesh` depends on `hex_editor` and the library cannot
// ask what is under the feet; the driver that has a mesher hands one in. This runner
// has no renderer and still needs the sampler, which is the seam working as designed.
use hex_mesh;

// ⚠ `HOUSE_W` / `HOUSE_D` WERE HERE AND ARE NOW `hex_editor`'s — plan 22 `W4`.
// This file and `src/editor_server.loft` each carried a copy; they agreed at 5×4
// and nothing made them. A parameter of what a key MEANS belongs beside the key.

// ⚠ THE SAME WORLD THE SERVER MAKES — AND FOR MONTHS THIS SAID SO WHILE BUILDING A
// DIFFERENT ONE. The sentence was right: *a scene built at a different epsilon is a
// different world however identical the script*. The two constants under it were
// `8` and `3` where the server used `10` and `4`. Measured 2026-08-13: the house
// scene at each differs in exactly two bytes, offsets 21 and 25 — `w_eps` and
// `w_theta` in the header, every cell equal — because nothing in `house.keys`
// stacks layers between 8 and 10 apart, so `F1` never fired differently.
//
// They are `hex_editor::WORLD_EPS` / `WORLD_THETA` now, for the reason `W_RESERVE`
// already was: what world this editor makes is a property of the gestures that
// write it, not of the program that happens to drive them.

fn seed_ground(w: VoxelWorld, half: integer) {
  for q in 0 - half..half + 1 {
    for r in 0 - half..half + 1 {
      hex_editor::ground_set(w, q, r, 0, hex_editor::SURFACE_MAT);
    }
  }
}

// ── THE WALK — plan 22 `T3` ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// How wide a window the collision proxy is built over, in hexes.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE SERVER'S 8 BECAUSE THE ACCEPTANCE IS BYTE-EQUALITY, AND THE SERVER'S
// 8 IS SIZED FOR A CAMERA THIS PROGRAM DOES NOT HAVE.** `editor_server.loft` says so
// itself — *"the walk needs one, the camera's boom needs four"* — so a runner picking
// its own number would be reasonable and would also be a second driver deciding what
// collision MEANS, which is the `LEVEL_R` finding one constant over. Copying the
// server's is the answer that cannot differ; whether a smaller one COULD is a
// measurement, and it is in `probe/t3/README.md` rather than in this comment.
const COLL_R = 8;

// A tick, and the only thing in this program that can produce one.
//
// ⚠ THE GROUND GOES IN AS A FUNCTION — the same closure the server hands over, for
// the cycle `tick.loft` records. ⚠ AND THE STRIDE IS `hex_editor::walker_step` OF THE
// WORLD'S OWN UNIT, not of a global: a part world is authored at 0.125, and what a
// walker can climb is a property of the figure against the world it stands in.
fn run_ticks(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, sess: EditSession, n: integer,
             last: &integer) {
  for _t in 0..n {
    hex_editor::walk_tick(wk, w, sess.es_runs, COLL_R,
                          hex_editor::walker_step(w.w_unit), hex_editor::LEVEL_R,
                          fn(gx: float, gz: float, gref: float) -> float {
                            hex_mesh::ground_under(w, gx, gz, gref) });
    say_mode(w, wk, sess, last);
  }
}

// ── THE MODE, LOGGED AND CONSULTED BY NOBODY — plan 22 `D1` ──────────────────
//
// [EDITING_MODES](../../doc/claude/EDITING_MODES.md)' `D1` in as many words: *computed,
// consulted by nobody, and a gate logs it every frame of an existing run.* This is that
// gate — `probe/k3d` keeps a committed record of every indented line of every live
// script, so the transitions below are baselined over the whole corpus the moment they
// exist, and a derivation that starts answering differently shows up as a diff.
//
// ⚠ **ON CHANGE, NOT EVERY TICK, AND THAT IS THE INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** A
// line per tick would put 90 identical words in the record for one `step 90` and drown
// the transition; a line per CHANGE makes the record say where the author crossed a
// threshold, which is the only thing a mode is about. The count is data — a script that
// walks in and out prints twice.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS CALLED FROM TWO PLACES BECAUSE THE AUTHOR MOVES IN TWO WAYS.** A tick
// walks; `at` and `send 7:` teleport. A log wired only to the tick would report nothing
// for the seven scripts that never walk at all, and would have read as *no script in
// this corpus is ever indoors*.
fn say_mode(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, sess: EditSession, last: &integer) {
  m = hex_editor::mode_at(sess, w, walk_author(wk));
  if m != last {
    println("  mode {hex_editor::mode_name(m)}");
    last = m;
  }
}

// Put the person at an exact pose — `at`, and the wire's `7:` PLACE.
//
// ⚠ **THE SERVER'S OWN TAIL, CLAUSE FOR CLAUSE** (`MSG_PLACE`): the feet are re-read
// from the ground unless levelling holds them at the frozen height, and the fall is
// cleared because a teleport is authoritative. ⏭ What the server does here and this
// does not is the ROAD and the WATER stamp, which hang off `7:` rather than off the
// tick — the one place a gesture still lives in a driver, and the reason `10:` and
// `47:` are still refused below.
fn walk_place(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, x: float, z: float, yaw: float) {
  wk.wk_x = x;
  wk.wk_z = z;
  wk.wk_yaw = yaw;
  if wk.wk_levelling { wk.wk_y = (wk.wk_level_h as float) * w.w_unit; }
  else { wk.wk_y = hex_mesh::ground_under(w, wk.wk_x, wk.wk_z, wk.wk_y); }
  wk.wk_vy = 0.0;
}

// Where a gesture is applied.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT USED TO BE `author_on`, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT HEIGHT — plan 22 `T3`.**
// `author_on` reads the CELL's stored height; the server builds its author from the
// walker, whose feet are `hex_mesh::ground_under`, interpolated across the cell.
// `gesture.loft` says what that difference is for: *a driver with no mesher cannot do
// better* — so it was never a bug, it was this runner being the coarse driver. It has a
// mesher now, for the fall, so it can give the same answer the server does.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE EFFECT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN CLAIMED, BECAUSE IT IS SMALLER THAN IT
// SOUNDS.** `probe/t3/height.loft`: the two functions differ over a raised cone at 726
// of 14641 samples, worst **0.166 wu** — and `fence`, `wall` and `stair_up`, the three
// verbs that read `au_y` at all, key the SAME world either way. The control is what
// makes that mean something: a whole STOREY apart does not move them either, because
// `au_y` chooses WHICH SURFACE you stand on and the fixture has one. So this is the
// runner agreeing with the server rather than a defect being closed, and the world that
// could tell them apart — a deck over the ground — is not one anything here builds yet.
fn walk_author(wk: hex_editor::Walker) -> Author {
  hex_editor::author_at(wk.wk_x, wk.wk_z, wk.wk_yaw, wk.wk_y)
}

// ── `plan <tag>` — THE PLAN VIEW, AT THIS TICK, WITH THE PERSON ON IT ───────
//
// Plan 26 `B3`. `src/plan_view.loft` draws a SAVED world and therefore has nobody to
// draw: the walker lives in whichever driver is running the tick. This is that
// driver, so this is where the author appears.
//
// ⚠ **THE POSE IT PRINTS IS READ BACK OUT OF THE PICTURE, NOT OFF THE WALKER.** A
// line that re-printed `wk_x` would compare the walker against the walker and pass
// for any drawing at all, including one that put the marker at the origin — the
// `CLAUDE.md` rule about measuring what was emitted, at the one place it is cheap to
// get wrong. `probe/plan` compares this line against `feet`, which IS the walker.
fn svg_attr(ln: text, name: text) -> text {
  sa_at = ln.find("{name}='");
  if sa_at == null { return ""; }
  sa_rest = ln[(((sa_at ?? 0) + name.size() + 2) as i32)..];
  sa_cut = sa_rest.find("'");
  if sa_cut == null { return ""; }
  sa_rest[0..(sa_cut ?? 0)]
}

// The window follows the AUTHOR rather than the origin, because a station shot is
// taken where the person is standing. `Q0`/`R0`/`Q1`/`R1` override it for a script
// that wants a fixed frame across stations.
const PLAN_R = 8;

// ⚠ **ONE WINDOW, SHARED BY `plan` AND `pick`** — plan 26 `B4b`. A pick resolves
// against *the plan I would draw right now*, so the two must derive the frame from one
// place or a click will land on a picture that was never drawn. The walker does not
// move during either, which is what makes "right now" a stable answer.
fn plan_window(wk: hex_editor::Walker, q0: &integer, r0: &integer,
               q1: &integer, r1: &integer) {
  (pw_cq, pw_cr) = hex_grid::px_to_hex(wk.wk_x, wk.wk_z);
  q0 = env_int("Q0", pw_cq - PLAN_R);
  r0 = env_int("R0", pw_cr - PLAN_R);
  q1 = env_int("Q1", pw_cq + PLAN_R + 1);
  r1 = env_int("R1", pw_cr + PLAN_R + 1);
}

fn run_plan(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, rest: text, said: &text) -> boolean {
  rp_name = env_variable("WORLD");
  if rp_name == "" { rp_name = "headless"; }
  rp_tag = rest.trim();
  if rp_tag != "" { rp_name = "{rp_name}-{rp_tag}"; }
  rp_q0 = 0;
  rp_r0 = 0;
  rp_q1 = 0;
  rp_r1 = 0;
  plan_window(wk, rp_q0, rp_r0, rp_q1, rp_r1);
  // One panel, at the feet: a station shot is *the floor I am standing on*. A script
  // that wants the storeys beside each other says so through `plan_view`, which reads
  // the world this run saves.
  rp_svg = plan_levels(w, rp_q0, rp_r0, rp_q1, rp_r1, [wk.wk_y],
                       plan_over_who(plan_author(walk_author(wk))), rp_name);
  rp_out = file("{source_dir()}/../worlds/{rp_name}.svg");
  rp_out.write(rp_svg);

  rp_x = "";
  rp_z = "";
  for rp_ln in rp_svg.split('\n') {
    if rp_ln.size() < 8 { continue; }
    if rp_ln[0..8] != "<circle " { continue; }
    rp_x = svg_attr(rp_ln, "cx");
    rp_z = svg_attr(rp_ln, "cy");
  }
  if rp_x == "" {
    said = "plan {rp_name}.svg drew no author — off-plan at {round(wk.wk_x * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0},{round(wk.wk_z * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0}";
    return true;
  }
  said = "plan {rp_name}.svg author {rp_x},{rp_z}";
  return true;
}

// ── `pick <x>,<y> <verb>` — AUTHOR AT A PAGE POSITION — plan 26 `B4b` ───────
//
// The plan's own gesture: a point on the picture, and a verb to apply there.
//
// ⚠ **A PICK IS A TARGET, NOT A TELEPORT, AND THAT IS WHY `Author` IS ITS OWN TYPE.**
// Clicking a plan to hang a door does not walk the person across the room — BLUEPRINT
// §3.1 keeps the character running its own way — so this builds an `Author` at the
// picked spot and hands it to the same `press_verb` every other spelling uses. The
// walker is not touched, and `probe/plan` checks that `feet` does not move.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE VERB IS THE VERB, NOT A SECOND TABLE.** `EDITING_MODES` counts four
// places that already decide what a key means; a plan-scale gesture that resolved its
// own would be the fifth. This spelling changes WHERE the gesture lands and nothing
// else about it.
fn run_pick(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, sess: hex_editor::EditSession,
            rest: text, said: &text) -> boolean {
  // ⚠ **TWO ARITIES, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS *LOOK* AGAINST *DO*** — plan 26 `B4c`.
  // `pick <x>,<y>` AIMS: it resolves the point, draws the plan with the spot marked on
  // it and authors nothing. With a verb it authors. A picture you can take before
  // committing is the whole of "see what you are about to author", and it is one word
  // rather than two because it is one question asked twice.
  rk_sp = rest.find(" ");
  rk_pt = rest.trim();
  rk_verb = "";
  if rk_sp != null {
    rk_pt = rest[..(rk_sp ?? 0)];
    rk_verb = rest[((rk_sp ?? 0) + 1)..].trim();
  }
  rk_ci = rk_pt.find(",");
  if rk_ci == null {
    said = "pick needs `<x>,<y>` — a page point has two numbers";
    return false;
  }
  rk_px = rk_pt[..(rk_ci ?? 0)] as float ?? 0.0;
  rk_py = rk_pt[((rk_ci ?? 0) + 1)..] as float ?? 0.0;

  rk_q0 = 0;
  rk_r0 = 0;
  rk_q1 = 0;
  rk_r1 = 0;
  plan_window(wk, rk_q0, rk_r0, rk_q1, rk_r1);
  rk_pk = plan_pick(rk_q0, rk_r0, rk_q1, rk_r1, [wk.wk_y], rk_px, rk_py);
  // ── AIM: draw it, author nothing ──────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // ⚠ **A REFUSED AIM IS STILL DRAWN, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF AIMING.** The picture
  // says `aim refused (in the gutter between levels)` in its own caption, which is the
  // answer to *why did nothing happen* that a silent no-op cannot give.
  if rk_verb == "" {
    rk_aname = env_variable("WORLD");
    if rk_aname == "" { rk_aname = "headless"; }
    rk_aname = "{rk_aname}-pick";
    rk_asvg = plan_levels(w, rk_q0, rk_r0, rk_q1, rk_r1, [wk.wk_y],
                          plan_over(plan_author(walk_author(wk)), rk_pk), rk_aname);
    file("{source_dir()}/../worlds/{rk_aname}.svg").write(rk_asvg);
    if !rk_pk.pk_ok {
      said = "aim ({rk_px},{rk_py}) refused — {rk_pk.pk_why} -> {rk_aname}.svg";
      return true;
    }
    said = "aim L{rk_pk.pk_level} ({rk_pk.pk_q},{rk_pk.pk_r}) at "
         + "{round(rk_pk.pk_x * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0},{round(rk_pk.pk_z * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0}"
         + " -> {rk_aname}.svg";
    return true;
  }

  // ⚠ A REFUSAL IS AN ANSWER AND THE LINE STILL SUCCEEDS. The point was understood
  // and named no cell; that is not a script error, and failing the run would make a
  // deliberate probe of the gutter indistinguishable from a typo.
  if !rk_pk.pk_ok {
    said = "pick ({rk_px},{rk_py}) refused — {rk_pk.pk_why}";
    return true;
  }
  rk_a = hex_editor::author_at(rk_pk.pk_x, rk_pk.pk_z, wk.wk_yaw, rk_pk.pk_ref);
  rk_ok = said_ack(hex_editor::press_verb(sess, w, rk_a, rk_verb,
                                          "{source_dir()}/../data/parts"),
                   rk_verb, said);
  said = "pick L{rk_pk.pk_level} ({rk_pk.pk_q},{rk_pk.pk_r}) at "
       + "{round(rk_pk.pk_x * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0},{round(rk_pk.pk_z * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0} · {said}";
  rk_ok
}

fn env_int(name: text, fallback: integer) -> integer {
  ei_v = env_variable(name);
  if ei_v == "" { return fallback; }
  ei_v as integer ?? fallback
}

// What a gesture did, in a driver's words — shared by `key` and `verb` so the two
// spellings cannot come to report differently — plan 22 `K1`.
//
// ⚠ NOT EVERY GESTURE HAS A SENTENCE. `raise_ahead` succeeds with an empty
// `ak_why`, and printing that verbatim made the press SILENT — which in a script's
// transcript reads as "the key did nothing", the one thing a driver must never say
// when something happened. A gesture without words gets what was ASKED and its
// count instead, which is why `what` is the caller's word rather than a key: a
// converted line says `place: 27` where the original said `H: 27`.
fn said_ack(ack: Ack, what: text, said: &text) -> boolean {
  if ack.ak_why == hex_editor::PR_NONE {
    said = "no gesture for {what}";
    return false;
  }
  if !ack.ak_ok { said = "refused: {ack.ak_why}"; return true; }
  said = ack.ak_why;
  if said == "" { said = "{what}: {ack.ak_n}"; }
  true
}

// ── THE SESSION, READ BACK — plan 22 `K1`, and it lives in `hex_editor` now ──
//
// ⚠ **THE WORLD IS BLIND TO EVERYTHING `K1` WAS ABOUT, AND THAT IS MEASURED
// RATHER THAN FEARED.** `open_ahead` writes `DOOR_MAT` whatever the profile, so a
// round-headed door and a pointed one leave worlds that are equal **byte for
// byte** (`V1`, and `probe/k1/run.sh` control `C`). The head lives in the
// session's `Opening`, and none of the session is in the world format (`S1`). So a
// script converted from `key P` to `select 1` + `verb opening` would build a
// provably identical world with the wrong arch in it, and every instrument this
// runner had would have said the conversion was lossless. The read-back is what
// makes converting a script an assertion instead of a hope.
//
// ⏭ **AND AT `B1b.1b` IT ACQUIRED A SECOND CONSUMER, SO IT MOVED.**
// `hex_editor::session_digest` is the same text, printed by this runner and by the
// page in local mode — because the thing being compared is two SESSIONS, and two
// programs each composing their own sentence would be comparing their agreement
// about a format. The reasons it prints the geometry rather than the kind, and the
// standing selection on its own line, are at the function.

// ── WHAT A `send` CARRIES — plan 22 `K3c` ────────────────────────────────────
//
// Which wire ids change nothing this runner builds, so that skipping one really does
// let a script serve both drivers. ⚠ **A WHITELIST, AND THE DEFAULT IS DENY** — the
// reasoning is at the `send` branch in `run_line`, and the short version is that a
// classifier which answers *harmless* to an id it has never seen is not a classifier.
//
// ⚠ **ENUMERATED, NOT DERIVED FROM THE CLASS LETTER.** `9` LOAD is filed **R** — a
// read-back — and replaces the whole store, so it is not here.
//
// ⚠ **AND `7` PLACE USED TO BE THE SECOND HALF OF THAT SENTENCE — plan 22 `T3`.** It is
// filed **X**, *a character pose, not world*, and it moves the datum every gesture
// reads, so it was refused for the same reason. It is PERFORMED now, in `run_send`
// beside `50:`: this runner walks, so it can move the author exactly rather than having
// to decline. The rule is unchanged and one of its two examples has been paid off.
fn send_quiet(id: integer) -> boolean {
  // **V** — the eye. Aspect, look, the rest report, the mode, and the placed eye.
  // Nothing here is read by a gesture; the runner has no renderer to point anyway.
  if id == 2 || id == 3 || id == 28 || id == 40 || id == 48 { return true; }
  // **R** — questions. A column, its walls, its dressing, one cell, the layer
  // labels, a part's sockets. The server answers them; nobody answers them here,
  // and an unanswered question writes nothing.
  if id == 15 || id == 16 || id == 20 || id == 26 || id == 29 || id == 45 {
    return true;
  }
  // **S**/**K** — the connection's own bookkeeping: the opening burst, the trace
  // switch, and the client saying it draws its own ground.
  if id == 1 || id == 27 || id == 43 { return true; }
  false
}

// Why a refused id is refused, in the words a person fixing a script needs. ⚠ **The
// four named first are the ones the live corpus actually sends**; the rest of the
// protocol falls to the last line, which is the honest answer for an id nobody here
// has thought about.
//
// ⛔ **THREE OF THESE SENTENCES SAID *"AND THIS RUNNER DOES NOT WALK"* AND THAT STOPPED
// BEING TRUE AT `T3`.** They are rewritten rather than deleted, because the refusals
// are still right and the REASON changed underneath them: what this runner lacks is no
// longer the walk, it is the stamp that hangs off the walk. Two of the three lack it
// for a reason that is worth the reader's attention — `road_lay` and `water_lay` are
// called from the SERVER's `7:` PLACE handler and are in no shared tick at all, so they
// are the gestures still written once, in a driver. `WALK_TICK.md` says exactly that in
// *what this design does NOT claim*, and this is where a script meets it.
fn send_why(id: integer) -> text {
  if id == 10 {
    return "`10:` ROAD stamps its grade from the SERVER's place handler rather than from the shared tick, so this runner walks and lays nothing";
  }
  if id == 47 {
    return "`47:` WATER lays its stream from the SERVER's place handler rather than from the shared tick, so this runner walks and lays nothing";
  }
  if id == 44 {
    return "`44:` PART swaps which store is edited, and this runner holds one world — so every gesture after it would land in the wrong one";
  }
  if id == 9 { return "`9:` LOAD replaces the store, and this runner builds the world it is given"; }
  "it writes, or nothing here has classified it; `send_quiet` is the list of ids known to change nothing"
}

// The plane every unwritten cell stands on — `E1γ` — and the one authoring message
// this runner performs rather than refuses.
//
// ⚠ **A REFUSAL RETURNS `true`, AS `said_ack` DOES.** The two answers this driver
// gives are not *good* and *bad*: they are *I did what the line asked* and *I could
// not read the line*. A ground below the reserve is the world declining a legal
// request, which is a result and belongs in the transcript — the same distinction
// `said_ack` draws between `PR_NONE` and a gesture that refused.
fn set_plane(w: VoxelWorld, clear: boolean, gh: integer, gm: integer, said: &text) -> boolean {
  cell = hex_voxel::Hex {};
  if !clear {
    cell = hex_voxel::Hex { h_height: gh as u16? ?? 0, h_material: gm as u8? ?? 0 };
  }
  gset = world_set_ground(w, cell);
  if gset.cw_code != CW_OK {
    said = "ground refused — {gset.cw_detail}";
    return true;
  }
  // ⚠ THE SERVER'S OWN SENTENCE, WORD FOR WORD (`MSG_PLANE`), and it is asked of the
  // WORLD rather than of the argument: `world_has_ground_default` is what knows
  // whether a ground is standing, so a cleared plane cannot report itself as set.
  if world_has_ground_default(w) { said = "ground {gh} material {gm}"; }
  else { said = "ground cleared"; }
  true
}

// `ground <height> <material>`, and `ground off` — the friendly spelling of `50:`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS AN AUTHORING MESSAGE AND IT IS PERFORMED RATHER THAN REFUSED**, which
// makes it the only one of its class here. `world_set_ground` is a call and not a
// tick, so this runner can do it exactly as the server does — and `E1γ` says what is
// at stake: the ground decides what every cell nobody wrote MEANS, so skipping it
// builds the whole scene on a different floor. Before `K3c` it was skipped, and
// `tools/scripts/plane.keys` — a script whose entire subject is the ground — keyed a
// world **byte-identical to one that does nothing but raise a hill**.
fn run_ground(w: VoxelWorld, rest: text, said: &text) -> boolean {
  // `ground off` and a bare `ground` clear it, which is `script.mjs`'s bare `50:`.
  if rest == "" || rest == "off" { return set_plane(w, true, 0, 0, said); }
  gsp = rest.find(" ");
  if gsp == null {
    said = "ground refused — say <height> <material>, or `off` to clear";
    return true;
  }
  set_plane(w, false, rest[..gsp] as integer ?? 0,
            rest[gsp + 1..].trim() as integer ?? 0, said)
}

// ── `send` IS WHATEVER IT CARRIES — plan 22 `K3c` ────────────────────────────
//
// ⛔ **`send` USED TO SIT ON THE SKIP LIST AND IT NEVER BELONGED THERE.** Those words
// are one thing each; `send <id>:<payload>` is a raw wire frame and is as many things
// as there are message ids. [WIRE_PROTOCOL.md](../doc/claude/WIRE_PROTOCOL.md) sorts
// every id into **A** (authoring — *survives as a store write*), **V** (view), **S**
// (session), **R** (read-back), **X** (dynamic state) and **D**/**K**; the skip list's
// words are V or S by construction and this is not.
//
// ⛔ **AND THE DROP WAS VISIBLE AS A COLLISION.** Measured over the live corpus on the
// commit before this: `doorparts`, `doorway`, `cache`, `slab` and the two `a83` probes
// all keyed **`32952:3318286153` at τ 3813** — six scripts, one world, because
// everything that made them different was inside a `44:` that never opened.
//
// ⚠ **THE CLASS LETTER IS A HINT AND NOT THE RULE, WHICH IS WHY `send_quiet` LISTS IDS
// AND NOT LETTERS.** The doc answers *what becomes of this message*; a driver asks
// *can this change what I build*, and two ids cross the two questions in opposite
// directions — `9` LOAD is filed **R** and replaces the whole store, `7` PLACE is filed
// **X**, explicitly *a character pose, not world*, and moves the datum every gesture
// reads. Whitelisting a letter waves both through.
//
// ⚠ **SO THE DEFAULT IS DENY.** Everything `send_quiet` does not name — including an id
// nobody has classified, including one this tree has not invented — **fails the run**.
// A classifier whose default answer is *harmless* is `CLAUDE.md`'s grep whose default
// answer is *absent*: it reports a clean result for every case it has never seen.
fn run_send(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, rest: text, said: &text) -> boolean {
  ci = rest.find(":");
  if ci == null { said = "send needs `<id>:<payload>`"; return false; }
  sid = rest[..ci].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1;
  pay = rest[ci + 1..].trim();
  // ── THE TWO MOVEMENT IDS ARE PERFORMED NOW — plan 22 `T3` ──────────────────
  //
  // ⚠ **BECAUSE THE WORD IS, AND A SPELLING MUST NOT DECIDE.** `keys 1` and
  // `send 4:1` are one operation with two names; performing one and refusing the
  // other is the four-site divergence rebuilt inside a single file. `50:` set the
  // precedent at `K3c` — an id this runner can genuinely do is done here, in
  // `run_send`, rather than whitelisted as harmless in `send_quiet`.
  if sid == 4 { wk.wk_held = pay as integer ?? 0; said = "keys {wk.wk_held}"; return true; }
  // ── `6:` LEVEL — plan 22 `T4`, and it is the last message `deck.keys` needed ──
  //
  // ⚠ **THE FREEZE AND THE RELEASE ARE THE LIBRARY'S, AND THE SENTENCE IS THE
  // DRIVER'S.** That split is `T1`'s: `level_on` answers the residual it quantised
  // away, `level_off` puts the feet back on the ground, and the STAMP is inside
  // `walk_tick` where a footfall reaches it. So this branch is two calls and two
  // sentences, and the pad `deck.keys` walks is laid by the tick rather than here.
  //
  // ⚠ **IT IS THE WORLD'S UNIT IN THE SENTENCE, NOT `hex_proj::HEIGHT_SCALE`.** The
  // server says `wk.wk_y / HEIGHT_SCALE` there; both read 0.25 on the landscape, and
  // `walk.loft`'s rule is that the global is wrong on a part world. A sentence is not
  // in the store, so this cannot move a world — which is exactly why it is the safe
  // place to prefer the right expression.
  if sid == 6 {
    if (pay as integer ?? 0) != 0 {
      lvres = 0.0;
      hex_editor::level_on(wk, w, lvres);
      said = "level true at height {wk.wk_level_h} "
           + "(quantised from {wk.wk_y / w.w_unit ?? 0.0}, residual {lvres})";
      return true;
    }
    hex_editor::level_off(wk, fn(gx: float, gz: float, gref: float) -> float {
                                hex_mesh::ground_under(w, gx, gz, gref) });
    said = "level false — feet back on the ground at {wk.wk_y}";
    return true;
  }
  if sid == 7 {
    // The server's own parse of `<x>,<z>,<yaw>`, and its own refusal: a payload
    // missing either comma moves nobody and says so.
    qi = pay.find(",");
    if qi == null { said = "place refused — say <x>,<z>,<yaw>"; return true; }
    ptail = pay[qi + 1..];
    qj = ptail.find(",");
    if qj == null { said = "place refused — say <x>,<z>,<yaw>"; return true; }
    walk_place(w, wk, pay[..qi] as float ?? wk.wk_x, ptail[..qj] as float ?? wk.wk_z,
               ptail[qj + 1..] as float ?? wk.wk_yaw);
    said = "placed {wk.wk_x},{wk.wk_z}";
    return true;
  }
  // ⚠ THE SAME PARSE THE SERVER'S `MSG_PLANE` BRANCH MAKES, deliberately — a comma,
  // and an empty payload meaning *clear*. A script may say either spelling of this one
  // message and must get the same floor; `probe/k3c` row D is what holds them together.
  if sid == 50 {
    if pay == "" { return set_plane(w, true, 0, 0, said); }
    pi = pay.find(",");
    if pi == null {
      said = "ground refused — say <height>,<material> or nothing to clear";
      return true;
    }
    return set_plane(w, false, pay[..pi] as integer ?? 0,
                     pay[pi + 1..].trim() as integer ?? 0, said);
  }
  if send_quiet(sid) { return true; }
  said = "send {sid}: is not something this runner can do — {send_why(sid)}. "
       + "Run this script through a server, or take the line out.";
  false
}

// One line of a script. Returns false only when the line asks for something this
// runner cannot do, which it says out loud rather than skipping.
//
// ⛔ **`walked` IS GONE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF `T3` RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP.** It held
// the first movement command this driver had SKIPPED since the last `at`, so that the
// `verb` which would have been applied at a stale author could refuse instead of
// landing somewhere wrong (plan 22 `K3e`). There is no skipped movement left: `keys`
// and `step` are performed, `hold` and `turn` are refused where they arrive, and a
// fence over an empty set is a fence over nothing. `probe/t3` is what checks that the
// thing the fence was protecting is now simply true.
fn run_line(w: VoxelWorld, wk: hex_editor::Walker, sess: EditSession, line: text,
            said: &text, last: &integer) -> boolean {
  t = line.trim();
  if t == "" || t[..1] == "#" { return true; }
  sp = t.find(" ");
  cmd = t;
  rest = "";
  if sp != null { cmd = t[..sp]; rest = t[sp + 1..].trim(); }

  if cmd == "echo" { said = rest; return true; }

  if cmd == "at" {
    // `at <x> <z> [yawdeg]` — the workhorse, and until `T3` the only way to be
    // anywhere. It is `7:` PLACE in friendly spelling, and it moves the WALKER now:
    // there is one datum for where the person is, and a teleport is a driver setting
    // it rather than a second answer beside it.
    p1 = rest.find(" ");
    if p1 == null { said = "at needs an x and a z"; return false; }
    xs = rest[..p1];
    tail = rest[p1 + 1..].trim();
    p2 = tail.find(" ");
    zs = tail;
    yaw = 0.0;
    if p2 != null {
      zs = tail[..p2];
      yaw = (tail[p2 + 1..].trim() as float ?? 0.0) * PI / 180.0 ?? 0.0;
    }
    walk_place(w, wk, xs as float ?? 0.0, zs as float ?? 0.0, yaw);
    said = "placed {wk.wk_x},{wk.wk_z}";
    return true;
  }

  // ── THE ONLY SPELLING — plan 22 `K1` opened it, `K3b` closed it ─────────────
  //
  // ⚠ **A KEY IS A KEYBOARD'S BUSINESS AND A VERB IS WHAT TRAVELS.** A script is a
  // document, not a keyboard: it outlives the layout it was written on, and it is
  // read by a person deciding what a scene does. `verb place` says that; `key H`
  // said which finger moved.
  //
  // ⛔ **AND `key <K>` IS GONE FROM THIS READER — plan 22 `K3b`.** It resolved through
  // `keymap_default()` for its last three steps and was the driver's whole reason to
  // know a keyboard existed. What it leaves behind is worth stating, because a
  // deletion's value is in what it makes impossible:
  //
  //   · `verb_in(keymap_default(), …)` had ONE production caller and it was this
  //     branch, so the default layout is no longer consulted by anything headless.
  //     The map is the client's now, where a person can rebind it (`M3`).
  //   · the eight keys `S3` collapsed — `P I U N M T K V` — stop being expressible
  //     at all rather than resolving to nothing. `probe/k2`'s grep that kept live
  //     scripts off them retires with the spelling it was policing.
  //   · a stale `key H` is an unknown word now, and `K3a` makes an unknown word
  //     **fail the run**. That guard is the floor this deletion stands on: without
  //     it the same line would be a silent no-op in a run reporting success.
  //
  // ⚠ **AND THE CONVERSION WAS NOT A SPELLING CHANGE FOR THE OPENING FAMILY.**
  // The definition is deliberately not injective: six keys named one `opening`, so a
  // script that says `opening` and never says `select` cuts whatever was chosen
  // last. That is why `select` landed in the same step as `K1` — a script converted
  // without it would still build a world, silently, with the wrong head on every
  // door. `lib/hex_editor/tests/opening.loft` holds the half of that rule the gates
  // used to: a gesture does not move the standing selection.
  if cmd == "verb" {
    // ⛔ **THIS IS WHERE A SKIPPED WALK USED TO BE PAID FOR, AND THE DEBT IS GONE —
    // plan 22 `T3`.** A gesture is applied AT the author, and until this step the
    // author was whatever the last `at` had said: a `verb` after a walk landed at the
    // teleport, so `K3e` refused it rather than let it build the wrong world quietly.
    // The walk is performed now, `walk_author` reads it off the walker, and the
    // refusal has nothing left to refuse. `probe/t3` row A is the same claim as
    // `probe/k3e` row A with its sign flipped — the gesture must land where the WALK
    // put the person, byte for byte with the server that walked it.
    //
    // ⚠ THE LIBRARY ROOT IS THIS RUNNER'S OWN — plan 22 `B2e`. It reads the same
    // `data/parts/` the server does, so a script that chooses a part and places it
    // builds what the editor would. ⚠ Resolved through `source_dir()` for loft#712's
    // reason, exactly as the SCRIPT path above is.
    return said_ack(hex_editor::press_verb(sess, w, walk_author(wk), rest,
                                           "{source_dir()}/../data/parts"), rest, said);
  }

  if cmd == "part" {
    // ⚠ THE SAME CHOOSING THE PAGE MAKES — plan 22 `B2e`. `select` picks an opening
    // profile; this picks a PART, and `verb place` then puts that in the world
    // instead of the procedural house. A bare `part` with no name clears it, which
    // is how a script goes back to building houses.
    sel = hex_editor::session_select_part(sess, rest);
    if !sel.ak_ok { said = "part refused — {sel.ak_why}"; return true; }
    said = sel.ak_why;
    return true;
  }

  if cmd == "select" {
    // ⚠ CHOOSING IS A GESTURE AND IT REFUSES LIKE ONE. `5` is not *nearly* an
    // oculus, so `session_select_open` says no without an offer and leaves the
    // standing choice alone — which is the server's `49:` behaviour, because it is
    // the same call. ⚠ A REACH IS THE EXCEPTION and refuses like an ordinal: 40 HAS
    // a nearest one, so `fit_ordinal` names it.
    //
    // ✅ **EVERY SUBJECT IS NAMED NOW — the bare form is gone.** It used to be the
    // fallthrough: `select 2` meant an opening, because `K2a` converted eight scripts
    // to write it that way and re-spelling them was judged not worth a migration. The
    // note here said to migrate it *"when a fourth selection lands"*; `reach` was the
    // fifth, and this is that migration.
    //
    // ⚠ **THE IMPLIED SUBJECT WAS NOT A TIDINESS PROBLEM, AND THE PROOF ARRIVED
    // BEFORE THE FIX DID.** `select reach 18` was added to THIS reader and not to
    // `tools/script.mjs`, whose fallthrough would have sent `49:reach` — an OPENING
    // selection carrying a word. A bare form does not merely permit that mistake; it
    // is the mechanism, because an unknown subject has somewhere to fall.
    //
    // ⚠ **SO AN UNKNOWN SUBJECT FAILS THE RUN** rather than choosing something. That
    // is `K3a`'s rule for every other word in this vocabulary — an unknown one is not
    // understood and the run exits non-zero — and `select` was the last word exempt.
    if rest.starts_with("opening ") {
      osel = hex_editor::session_select_open(sess, rest[8..].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1);
      if !osel.ak_ok { said = "selection refused — {osel.ak_why}"; return true; }
      said = "opening {osel.ak_n} selected";
      return true;
    }
    if rest.starts_with("reach ") {
      rsel = hex_editor::session_select_reach(sess, rest[6..].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1);
      if !rsel.ak_ok { said = "selection refused — {rsel.ak_why}"; return true; }
      said = "reach {rsel.ak_n} selected";
      return true;
    }
    if rest.starts_with("seat ") {
      ssel = hex_editor::session_select_seat(sess, rest[5..].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1);
      if !ssel.ak_ok { said = "selection refused — {ssel.ak_why}"; return true; }
      said = "seat {ssel.ak_n} selected";
      return true;
    }
    if rest.starts_with("annex ") {
      asel = hex_editor::session_select_annex(sess, rest[6..].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1);
      if !asel.ak_ok { said = "selection refused — {asel.ak_why}"; return true; }
      said = "annex {asel.ak_n} selected";
      return true;
    }
    // ⚠ **A WALL TYPE IS AN EDGE PALETTE SLOT — plan 26 `B4e`** — so this is the
    // subject that pairs with `declare edge <slot> …` two branches down, and the pair
    // is what lets one script stand two kinds of wall in one world. It does NOT check
    // that the slot is declared: `session_select_wall`'s own note says why, and the
    // picture is what shows the difference.
    if rest.starts_with("wall ") {
      wsel = hex_editor::session_select_wall(sess, rest[5..].trim() as integer ?? 0 - 1);
      if !wsel.ak_ok { said = "selection refused — {wsel.ak_why}"; return true; }
      said = "wall {wsel.ak_n} selected";
      return true;
    }
    said = "select needs a subject: opening, reach, seat, annex or wall";
    return false;
  }

  // `wait`, `snap` and `rate` are the SERVER's vocabulary: they wait for an
  // acknowledgement, photograph a renderer, or set a clock, and none of those exists
  // here. Skipped rather than refused, so one script serves both.
  //
  // ⚠ **`rate` STAYS ON THIS LIST AND `step` HAS LEFT IT — plan 22 `T3`, and the pair
  // is the design in one line.** A tick is what a script asks for; a RATE is how a
  // driver decides to give it, and this one has no clock to set. `rate 0` is exactly
  // what this runner already is, which is why `T0` converting the walking scripts to
  // it is what made them expressible here at all.
  // ⚠ `eye` IS HERE FOR THE SAME REASON `snap` IS: it photographs, and there is no
  // renderer in this program. A camera placed where nobody is looking is not a
  // refusal, it is a verb this driver has no use for — so one script still serves
  // both, which is the whole reason this skip list exists.
  //
  // ⛔ **THIS LIST WAS STALE AND THE STALENESS WAS INVISIBLE — plan 22 `K3a`.** It
  // named nine words and MISSED nine more that real scripts use, and a missed word
  // is reported in exactly the same sentence a typo is: `unknown command '<x>'`,
  // printed, survived, **exit 0**. Measured over the whole corpus before this line
  // moved — **25 of 55 scripts** printed at least one, `send` in 22 of them. So the
  // driver could not tell *not my vocabulary* from *not a word*, and neither answer
  // reached the exit code. That is what made plan 22 `K3` unsafe as written: delete
  // the `key` branch above with this line as it was, and a stale `key H` becomes a
  // silent no-op in a run that still says it succeeded.
  //
  // ⚠ **THE SIX BELOW ARE PICTURE AND TIMING, AND THAT IS WHY THEY ARE SAFE TO
  // SKIP.** `mesh`/`meshr` count what a renderer built, `frame` judges the last
  // `snap`, `cam` and `feet` read where the eye and the walker are, `last` reads a
  // running tally off the wire and `until` waits for one. Not one of them writes a
  // cell, so a script serves both drivers across them exactly as the design claims.
  //
  if cmd == "wait" || cmd == "snap" || cmd == "rate" || cmd == "save"
     || cmd == "eye" || cmd == "mesh" || cmd == "meshr" || cmd == "frame"
     || cmd == "cam" || cmd == "last" || cmd == "until" {
    return true;
  }

  // ── `feet` IS PERFORMED, AND IT IS AN INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN A GESTURE ──────
  //
  // ⛔ **IT WAS ON THE SKIP LIST ABOVE, DEFENDED AS *IT ONLY READS WHERE THE WALKER
  // IS* — and that stopped being harmless the moment the walker moved.** Where the
  // person ENDED is the only thing that can tell a walk which was blocked from one
  // that ran free, and a saved world cannot: measured, a fence ring changes the world
  // whether or not it stops anybody, because the fence's own 42 cells are in it. Asked
  // whether the collision proxy's reach matters, the world answered *no* at every reach
  // from 8 down to 1 — and it answers exactly the same to a walk that never met the
  // fence at all. That is `probe/t1`'s probe 4 arriving one step later: **a fixture
  // that cannot see its subject says what a subject that does not exist says.**
  //
  // With `feet` the question is answerable, and the answer is a number: the fence
  // stops the walk at `4.32,2.5` against `8.32,4.8` free, and it stops it there at
  // reach 8, 4, 2 and 1 alike.
  //
  // ⚠ **THE VALUES AGREE WITH THE SERVER'S AND THE RENDERING DOES NOT, WHICH IS WORTH
  // SAYING PRECISELY RATHER THAN CLAIMING A MATCH.** `script.mjs` pads — `toFixed(3)`
  // and `toFixed(2)` — so `cellar.keys`'s five stations read `feet 4.250 at 17.00,0.00`
  // there and `feet 4.25 at 17,0` here. Measured against a native server, all five
  // agree to every digit either side prints: 4.25, 4.25, 3.25, 2.25, 1.25 at 17, 22.5,
  // 20.8, 19, 17. It reads the body's model matrix there and the walker here, which is
  // the same number by construction now that the walker IS the pose — so a transcript
  // is comparable by VALUE and not by `diff`.
  //
  // ⏭ Its judging form — `feet <lo> <hi>`, which fails a run — is not here; this
  // reports, and nothing in the corpus judges through this driver yet.
  // ⚠ **THE WALKER'S OWN NUMBERS, PRINTED IN EXACTLY ONE PLACE UNTIL NOW.**
  // `wk_fell` is a field on `hex_editor::Walker` — the same walker this program holds
  // — and the only line in the tree that ever printed it was the browser client's
  // summary at `editor_client.loft`. So *a fall completed* was a browser-only claim by
  // accident of where a `println` was written, not because the fact needs a browser:
  // `probe/b2`'s `H` block rebuilt a 7 MB wasm client and drove a headless Chromium to
  // read a counter this program has had all along.
  //
  // ⚠ A SEPARATE COMMAND RATHER THAN A WIDER `feet`. `cellar.keys` and
  // `threshold.keys` already print `feet`, and widening a line two committed scripts
  // emit is a change to their output for no gain — this one is additive and nothing
  // can have depended on it.
  //
  // ⚠ THE SAME FIELDS AND THE SAME ORDER AS THE CLIENT'S LINE, so the two are
  // comparable by eye and by `sed`. A headless report in a different shape would make
  // every comparison a translation.
  if cmd == "walker" {
    said = "walker yaw {wk.wk_yaw} walked {wk.wk_walked} "
         + "at ({round(wk.wk_x * 1000.0) / 1000.0 ?? 0.0},"
         + "{round(wk.wk_z * 1000.0) / 1000.0 ?? 0.0}) "
         + "feet {wk.wk_y} landed {wk.wk_fell}";
    return true;
  }

  if cmd == "feet" {
    said = "feet {round(wk.wk_y * 1000.0) / 1000.0 ?? 0.0} "
         + "at {round(wk.wk_x * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0},{round(wk.wk_z * 100.0) / 100.0 ?? 0.0}";
    return true;
  }

  // ── THE WALK — plan 22 `T3` ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // `keys <bits>` holds W=1 S=2 A=4 D=8, `step <n>` advances exactly n ticks. Those
  // two words are the whole of walking, and between them they are what `deck.keys`
  // needs: a held key changes nothing on its own, and every tick this program takes
  // is asked for by a line of the script.
  //
  // ⚠ **`keys` PRODUCES NO TICK, AND THAT IS THE SERVER'S BEHAVIOUR RATHER THAN A
  // SIMPLIFICATION.** `tools/script.mjs` sends `4:<bits>` and sleeps 60 ms; at
  // `rate 0` the server's `may_tick` is `sim_pending > 0`, so that sleep produces no
  // ticks at all. `T0` measured it — the walk in `deck.keys` is exactly 90 ticks *by
  // construction* — and it is the reason a `rate 0` script can be exact here.
  if cmd == "keys" {
    wk.wk_held = rest as integer ?? 0;
    said = "keys {wk.wk_held}";
    return true;
  }
  if cmd == "step" {
    run_ticks(w, wk, sess, rest as integer ?? 0, last);
    said = "stepped {rest as integer ?? 0}";
    return true;
  }

  // ── ⛔ `hold` AND `turn` ARE REFUSED, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT ────────
  //
  // Both are FEEDBACK LOOPS ON A LIVE POSE, not commands. `tools/script.mjs` sends
  // `4:<bit>`, then awaits ticks one at a time and reads the character's model matrix
  // off the wire after each, stopping when the distance covered or the angle
  // accumulated passes what was asked. So what they mean is *keep going until the
  // server says you have arrived* — and this runner is the server, which makes the
  // loop a thing it would be inventing rather than reproducing.
  //
  // ⛔ **AND AT `rate 0` THEY ARE NO-OPS ON THE SERVER, WHICH IS WHAT SETTLES IT.**
  // `nextT()` returns false when no tick arrives inside its limit, and at `rate 0` no
  // tick ever arrives unasked — so both loops break on their first iteration and the
  // whole command is `4:<bit>` followed by `4:0`. A runner that performed them as
  // exact loops would walk where the driver it is compared against stood still, and
  // `T0` has just pinned every walking script to `rate 0`. Refusing is the only
  // answer that does not manufacture a divergence.
  //
  // ⚠ **AND IT COSTS NOTHING TODAY, WHICH IS ALSO MEASURED**: `hold` and `turn` have
  // **zero** callers in the whole corpus — `tools/scripts/*.keys` and `probe/*/*.keys`
  // between them say `keys` 32 times and neither of these words once. `script.mjs`'s
  // own comment already records that it keeps them with no callers.
  if cmd == "hold" || cmd == "turn" {
    said = "`{t}` is a feedback loop on a live pose — it holds a key and reads the "
         + "character's matrix off the wire until the distance or the angle is met, "
         + "which needs a server to read back from. At `rate 0` it is a no-op even "
         + "there. Say `keys <bits>` and `step <n>`, which is exact on both drivers.";
    return false;
  }

  // ── `send` AND `ground` ARE CLASSIFIED — plan 22 `K3c` ──────────────────────
  // Both are one line here and a function below, because the reasoning is long and
  // `run_line` is already at the complexity the compiler nudges at.
  if cmd == "plan"   { return run_plan(w, wk, rest, said); }
  if cmd == "pick"   { return run_pick(w, wk, sess, rest, said); }
  if cmd == "ground" { return run_ground(w, rest, said); }
  if cmd == "send"   { return run_send(w, wk, rest, said); }
  // ⚠ **`declare` IS THE WAY A TYPE GETS INTO A WORLD AT ALL — plan 22 `T1c`.**
  // Before it, `hex_voxel::world_set_palette` had zero production callers, so a house
  // type could be read by `press_verb` and written by nothing but a loft test.
  //
  // ⚠ **THE WHOLE LINE GOES TO THE LIBRARY**, exactly as it does on the socket: one
  // parser, one refusal set, one sentence. `S2c` measured what the alternative costs.
  //
  // ⚠ **AND A REFUSED DECLARATION FAILS THE RUN**, which `run_ground` beside it does
  // not do. That asymmetry is deliberate and it is `K3a`'s rule, not an oversight: a
  // declaration that did not land leaves the script building the DEFAULT house while
  // reading as though it had asked for a castle — the silent-drop class `K3c` was
  // written for. `ground`'s own refusal predates that rule and is a separate row.
  if cmd == "declare" {
    (dcq, dcr) = hex_grid::px_to_hex(wk.wk_x, wk.wk_z);
    dack = hex_editor::world_declare(w, hex_voxel::world_region_at(w, dcq, dcr), rest);
    said = dack.ak_why;
    return dack.ak_ok;
  }

  said = "unknown command '{cmd}'";
  false
}

fn main() {
  // ⚠ RESOLVED THROUGH `source_dir()`, and that is loft#712 rather than taste. A
  // RELATIVE path with `..` in it is refused and reported as a null size, and a
  // relative path without one resolves against THIS FILE's directory — so a plain
  // `tools/scripts/house.keys` is looked for in `src/` and comes back missing. An
  // absolute path built from `source_dir()` is the one spelling that works.
  script = env_variable("SCRIPT");
  if script == "" { script = "tools/scripts/house.keys"; }
  if script[..1] != "/" { script = "{source_dir()}/../{script}"; }
  name = env_variable("WORLD");
  if name == "" { name = "headless"; }

  src = file(script);
  if src#size < 1 {
    println("editor_run: cannot read {script}");
    return;
  }
  text = src.content();

  w = world_new(0.25, hex_editor::W_RESERVE,
                hex_editor::WORLD_EPS, hex_editor::WORLD_THETA).wn_world;
  // ⚠ THE GROUND THIS RUNNER LAYS IS THE RUNNER'S, NOT THE EDITOR'S — plan 22
  // `B1b.1b`. `new_world()` in the server seeds nothing, because an unwritten cell
  // reads as absent and absence IS the floor (`E1γ`); this patch of `SURFACE_MAT`
  // exists so a scripted scene has something with a material in it to photograph.
  // That makes it a difference between two drivers of the same gestures, and the
  // page in local mode is the third — so it is a knob rather than a constant.
  //
  // ⚠ `GROUND=0` IS THE COMPARISON, and it is not a test hook: it is *start where
  // the editor starts*. Measured — the same six verbs at the same pose leave
  // τ 4079 seeded and τ 358 not, and the world keys differ, so a comparison run
  // against the default would report a divergence that is entirely this line.
  gh = env_variable("GROUND");
  half = if gh == "" { 30 } else { gh as integer ?? 30 };
  if half > 0 { seed_ground(w, half); }
  // ⚠ ONE WALKER, AND THE AUTHOR IS DERIVED FROM IT — plan 22 `T3`. It is the third
  // driver to hold a `hex_editor::Walker`, after the server and the page, and the
  // first for which the walker is the ONLY record of where the person is: there is no
  // camera here to disagree with it and no wire to re-send it over.
  wk = hex_editor::walker_new(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
  // The feet start on the ground rather than at zero, which is `walk_place`'s own
  // clause — a runner whose first line is `verb` rather than `at` must still be
  // standing on something.
  walk_place(w, wk, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
  // ⚠ AN `EditSession`, NOT A BARE `WallRuns` — plan 22 `W4`. A key press is a
  // gesture applied to a SESSION, and the server has held one since the headless
  // thread landed. A runner holding only the wall runs could never call `press`
  // for any gesture that touches another registry.
  sess = hex_editor::EditSession {};

  n = 0;
  // ⛔ **A LINE THIS RUNNER DID NOT UNDERSTAND USED TO PRINT AND SURVIVE — plan 22
  // `K3a`.** `run_line` has said `false` since it was written and main threw the
  // answer away: the complaint went to stdout, the loop carried on, and the process
  // exited **0**. So a script could lose a gesture and still report success, which
  // is the shape `CLAUDE.md` calls a grep whose default answer is *absent*. It is
  // counted now, and the count is a premise rather than a diagnostic.
  bad = 0;
  // ⚠ **SEEDED FROM THE MODE THE RUN STARTS IN, NOT FROM A SENTINEL — plan 22 `D1`.**
  // A `-1` would make every script announce `mode outside` on its first line, which is
  // 30 identical lines saying *the editor starts in a field*: true, and not news. What
  // the record is for is the CROSSINGS, so the opening state is the baseline rather
  // than the first event. ⚠ It also means a script that is never anything but outside
  // prints nothing at all, and that silence is the honest report.
  mode_last = hex_editor::mode_at(sess, w, walk_author(wk));
  for line in text.split('\n') {
    said = "";
    ok = run_line(w, wk, sess, line, said, mode_last);
    if said != "" { println("  {said}"); }
    // ⚠ AFTER THE LINE'S OWN SENTENCE, so the record reads *what was done* and then
    // *where that left the author* — a gesture can move the mode without moving the
    // person: placing a house around yourself is the case.
    say_mode(w, wk, sess, mode_last);
    if !ok { println("editor_run: {line}"); bad = bad + 1; }
    n = n + 1;
  }

  println(session_digest(sess));

  // The world as one comparable string — plan 22 `B1b.1b`. It is `hex_voxel`'s
  // function rather than this file's, because the other side of the comparison is
  // a page in a browser: two programs each spelling their own digest is `W1`'s
  // two-encoders finding one layer out, and this one exists precisely so that a
  // difference between them means a difference in what they BUILT.
  // ⚠ **THE KEY COMES OUT OF THE SAVE, WHICH IS ONE SERIALISATION RATHER THAN TWO.**
  // `world_key` encodes the whole world and `world_save` encodes it again — measured
  // natively at ~24 ms each on a 49x49 world, of which ~21 ms is the encode. This ran
  // both on every scripted run, and `probe/k3d` makes 34 of them.
  //
  // ⚠ **AND IT IS NOW THE KEY OF THE BYTES THAT REACHED THE DISK**, not of a second
  // encode of the same world. The two could not disagree before either — same function,
  // same world — but "could not disagree" was an argument, and this is a fact.
  path = "{source_dir()}/../worlds/{name}.hxw";
  wkey = "";
  code = world_save_keyed(w, path, [], hex_voxel::OWNER_ANY, wkey);
  // ⚠ PRINTED IN THE SAME PLACE IT ALWAYS WAS, before the summary line. The record in
  // `probe/k3d` reads named fields rather than positions, but `probe/headless` greps
  // this transcript, and a step that reorders a log to save 21 ms is not a saving.
  println("editor_run: world {wkey}");
  println("editor_run: {n} lines, {len(w.w_chunks)} chunks, τ {w.w_tau} -> {name}.hxw (code {code})");

  // ⚠ **AFTER THE SAVE AND AFTER EVERY DIAGNOSTIC, DELIBERATELY — plan 22 `K3a`.**
  // Failing earlier would leave `worlds/{name}.hxw` holding *the previous run's*
  // world, which is the worse artifact: it has no relationship to this run at all,
  // and it reads as fresh. Saving first means the file, the digest and the world key
  // above all describe the same run — the one that lost some lines — and the exit
  // code is what says so. A caller that checks rc gets the truth; a caller that does
  // not at least gets a file that matches the log it just read.
  //
  // ⚠ **AND IT REPORTS THE COUNT RATHER THAN THE FIRST OFFENDER.** Every bad line
  // was already printed above with `editor_run: <line>`, so a person fixing a script
  // sees all of them in one run instead of one per run — the loop is the diagnostic,
  // this is only the verdict.
  assert(bad == 0, "{bad} of {n} lines were not understood — each is printed above after `editor_run:`. A word this runner skips on purpose is on the skip list in `run_line`; anything else is a typo or a command only the server has.");
}
